# Copy to .env before `docker compose up -d`.
#
# TWO TOKEN BUCKETS, NAMED FOR WHAT THEY GRANT.
#
#   CASEBROKER_WRITE_TOKENS  read AND write -- lease, heartbeat, complete, fail,
#                            release, add cases. This is what a worker carries,
#                            and what site_sampler.py needs to publish cases.
#   CASEBROKER_READ_TOKENS   read ONLY -- status, case list, one case. Rejected
#                            with 401 by every mutating endpoint, so this is what
#                            a shared dashboard link can safely hand out.
#
# A write token also reads; you do not need both to operate. Each is a
# comma-separated list, which is how you rotate without stranding a worker
# mid-lease: add the new token, move the workers over, then drop the old one.
#
# Generate one (no install needed beyond this package):
#   uv run casebroker token new
#
# Ask a running broker what a token can actually do:
#   uv run casebroker token check --broker https://... --token <TOKEN>
#   uv run casebroker health --broker https://...
#
# LEAVING BOTH UNSET TURNS AUTH OFF ENTIRELY -- every caller gets write. That is
# fine for a laptop smoke test and never for anything reachable; `casebroker
# health` exits non-zero and says so, and /healthz reports "auth": "OPEN".
CASEBROKER_WRITE_TOKENS=replace-me-with-a-long-random-token
CASEBROKER_READ_TOKENS=

# Deprecated spellings, still honoured so existing deployments keep working:
#   CASEBROKER_TOKENS           -> CASEBROKER_WRITE_TOKENS
#   CASEBROKER_READONLY_TOKENS  -> CASEBROKER_READ_TOKENS
# Setting a variable and its deprecated twin to DIFFERENT values is refused at
# startup rather than resolved by precedence -- otherwise a token you believe you
# revoked can quietly keep working.

BROKER_DOMAIN=broker.example.org
